<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 02: Apportionment and Representation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete the exercises below and explain your reasoning clearly.
      Use the 2020 census or a provided sample dataset to compare apportionment methods and their fairness implications.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Congressional Apportionment</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The U.S. House of Representatives has 435 seats. The apportionment method determines how many seats each state receives after the decennial census.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain why apportionment is a governance decision and not just an arithmetic exercise.
              What constitutional principles does it serve, and what kinds of unfair outcomes may still arise?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the standard divisor and quotas for the following simplified population data:
            </p>
            <table>
              <title>States and Populations</title>
              <tabular>
                <row><cell>State</cell><cell>Population</cell></row>
                <row><cell>A</cell><cell>5,000,000</cell></row>
                <row><cell>B</cell><cell>3,200,000</cell></row>
                <row><cell>C</cell><cell>1,800,000</cell></row>
                <row><cell>D</cell><cell>1,100,000</cell></row>
                <row><cell>E</cell><cell>800,000</cell></row>
              </tabular>
            </table>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Apply Jefferson's method, Webster's method, and Huntington-Hill to this data. Compare the seat allocations and explain any differences.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each method, identify whether it violates the quota rule and describe the governance consequence of that violation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Reflect on an international example: how would a parliament with 100 seats and proportional lists differ from a U.S.-style single-member district House? What fairness trade-offs are involved?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Paradox Detection</title>
      <statement>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain the Alabama Paradox and the Population Paradox in your own words.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Give a numerical example showing the Alabama Paradox under the Hamilton method.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Why do these paradoxes matter for modern democratic governance?
              In particular, discuss the implications for public trust and for states whose population changes between censuses.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
